**8. Cleanup** (Bash, foreground)
```bash
herdr tab close <wgka tab_id>
git -C ~/work/ccs.ansible worktree remove .claude/worktrees/wg-keepalive
git -C ~/work/ccs.ansible worktree remove .claude/worktrees/wg-keepalive-docs
git -C ~/work/ccs.ansible worktree prune
git -C ~/work/ccs.ansible branch -d feat/wg-keepalive-relay chore/wg-keepalive-docs
kill 48211
git -C ~/work/ccs.ansible pull && git -C ~/work/ccs.ansible worktree list
```
Then backlog `done`, republish, and the next status.

## Why

- **Acceptance is not `done`.** Docs closed and cleanup are gates (§8). The stale snapshot matters tonight: the RDP audio advisors read the repo and would otherwise design against a pre-relay ccs-it.
- **"Paperwork at any time" is how it never gets done.** After nine hours my own context is the least reliable store, so the memory note is written now.
- **The snapshot goes through a worktree.** A prose subagent writes it from my evidence, it is committed, and it merges as docs-only, which needs no vote.
- **`wgka` is closed, not reused.** One tab is one task (§4). RDP audio is a new task, so it starts at stage 0 and no executor exists until `brainstormed`. What `wgka` knows about the roles is in the repo.
- **The voters do not draft.** A voter that drafted would judge its own work (§10a), so there are two separate product-advisor tabs.
- **I design nothing.** The brief carries quoted words and verified facts only.
- **"By morning" is a wish.** It waives no gate, and the brainstorm has no time box. No poll goes out now because no conflict exists yet: the full brainstorm may fit overnight. If it cannot, that is a change of course and the operator's poll.
- **Nothing waits on anything else.** Both streams run as background calls.
